            };
            elenchi["en"]["C4"] = new string[] {
                "## Moon position",
                "The geocentric position of the Moon is computed with the ELP-2000/82 theory in the abridged form by J. Meeus (Astronomical Algorithms, ch. 47): longitude, latitude and distance from the periodic series, with the dynamical time correction (Delta T). Then come nutation and the obliquity of the ecliptic, the conversion to equatorial coordinates, the apparent sidereal time and the topocentric parallax correction (ch. 40), with the Earth ellipsoid and the station height. The accuracy is about 0.01°.",
                "The Maidenhead locator (4, 6, 8 or 10 characters) is converted to the centre of its square. Azimuth is counted from North towards East. Atmospheric refraction, when enabled, is computed with Sæmundsson's formula R = 1.02 / tan(h + 10.3/(h + 5.11)) arcminutes; otherwise the elevation is geometric, as in the usual EME programs.",
                "## Pointability and windows",
                "Every instant is compared with the antenna limits (clockwise azimuth sector and elevation range) of your station and, when enabled, of the DX: green = pointable, orange = outside the limits, grey = below the horizon. Useful and common windows are the consecutive intervals of pointable instants.",
                "## Doppler and CFOM",
                "The one-way Doppler between station and Moon is -f·(dR/dt)/c, with the radial velocity dR/dt computed by difference over ±30 s from the topocentric distance. The own echo has twice the shift; between two stations the shift is the sum of the two. In CFOM you transmit on f0 - Doppler and receive on f0 + Doppler.",
                "## Polarization, Dpol and MNR",
                "The local polarization angle is computed as in WSJT-X from the latitude and from the azimuth and elevation of the Moon; Dpol is the difference between the angles of the two stations, between -90° and +90°. The linear polarization loss is -20·log|cos Dpol| (at most 30 dB) and the maximum non-reciprocity MNR = -20·log|cos 2·Dpol| (at most 25 dB). Faraday rotation cannot be predicted and is not included.",
                "## Sky noise and Dgrd",
                "The sky temperature behind the Moon (Tsky) at 144 MHz is taken along the ecliptic from a table in 2° steps (WSJT-X) and, in the Sky view, from a 2° all-sky map derived from the Global Sky Model; other frequencies are scaled with spectral index 2.6: T(f) = (T144 - 2.7)·(144/f)^2.6 + 2.7 K. Dgrd, as in WSJT-X, adds the effect of distance, -40·log(d / 356 903 km), and that of noise, -10·log((Tsky + 80 K) / (Tskymin + 80 K)), with Tskymin = 13·(408/f)^2.6 K.",
                "## EME budget",
                "The path loss follows the radar equation: L = 10·log((4·pi)^3·d1²·d2² / (sigma·lambda²)), with sigma = 0.065·pi·R² (lunar reflectivity 0.065, lunar radius 1737.4 km) and d1, d2 the topocentric distances of the two stations at the chosen instant: the variation between perigee and apogee (about 2.3 dB) is therefore included. EIRP is P minus cable and accessory losses, plus G + 10·log N and any ground gain.",
                "The noise figure of the receiving chain (losses, preamplifier, cable, transverter, patch cables, radio) is computed with the Friis formula; that of the radio is derived from its MDS: NF = MDS + 174 - 10·log B. The system temperature is Tsys = Tant + (Tsky - reference Tsky, if positive) + Tsun + receiver Te, where Tant is the antenna temperature from the VE7BQH tables (antenna tilted at 30°) and the reference is 200 K at 144 MHz scaled with index 2.6. Noise is k·Tsys·B and S/N is the signal at the antenna minus the noise in the chosen bandwidth.",
                "With \"BETA- antenna without elevation\" the ground gain of a horizontally polarized antenna at height h is 20·log|2·sin(2·pi·h·sin(el)/lambda)| (at most +6 dB), computed at the Moon elevation.",
                "## Solar noise",
                "The solar fluxes measured by the RSTN network (245, 410, 610, 1415, 2695, 4995, 8800 and 15400 MHz) are interpolated on a logarithmic scale to the working frequency, or extrapolated with a power law below 245 MHz and above 15400 MHz. The Sun is treated as an unpolarized point source: T = S·Ae/(2k), with the effective area Ae derived from the gain and attenuated by the antenna pattern (Gaussian lobe with width estimated from the gain, then side lobes at the set level). The Sun position is computed with Meeus, ch. 25.",
                "## Sky view, map and height",
                "The Sky view draws the local sky with the Tsky colours, the antenna limits, the DX horizon, the Sun, the Moon and the main radio sources. The map uses the Mercator projection and the OpenStreetMap, OpenTopoMap or CARTO tiles, saved in data\\tiles; the worked squares are read from the ADIF file, also downloaded from LoTW. With a 10-character locator the ground height is read from Open Topo Data (EU-DEM 25 m in Europe, SRTM 30 m elsewhere) or, as an alternative, from Open-Meteo (Copernicus DEM GLO-90). Local time uses the Windows time zones chosen from the position.",
                "## Sun near the Moon",
                "For each day of the Distance and Dgrd tab, the minimum angular separation between Sun and Moon is computed over the useful hours with the Moon above the horizon and compared with the receiving beamwidth estimated from the gain, θ ≈ √(41253/G): within 3 beamwidths the day is marked in red, within 6 in orange. The noise increase itself is estimated in the Solar noise tab.",
                "## Polar and auroral absorption",
                "The geomagnetic latitude of each station is computed with the centred dipole of IGRF-14 (2025 north geomagnetic pole at 80.8° N, 72.8° W): sin φm = sin φ·sin φp + cos φ·cos φp·cos(λ - λp). A station beyond the polar circles (|φ| ≥ 66.56°) or with |φm| > 75° is in the polar cap (possible PCA); with 60° ≤ |φm| ≤ 75° it is in the auroral zone. The warning appears when a locator is changed and stays on the main screen.",
                "## Tabs, Reconnaissance of my obstacles and Calculation check",
                "The tabs are grouped on two levels (groups and tabs of the group), drawn by the program above the pages. The Reconnaissance of my obstacles projects the local sky onto the map (horizon = outer circle, zenith = centre, radius proportional to 90° - elevation) and can blur the zones outside the limits; in the Sky view the same zones are covered with light grey diagonal lines. The Calculation check tab repeats the whole Budget EME and Solar noise calculation step by step, with the formulas, the substituted values and the results."
            };
